tests/syscalls — the pinned per-op syscall surface. Tier 10a, tools/bscalls.sh.

One file per case per platform, holding the calls that case made BEYOND the
broker's own plumbing, counted, from the fork onwards. Most of them are empty,
and an empty file is the strongest statement in this directory: that fixture
asked the kernel for nothing at all.

The two directories are not expected to agree, and that is the point of having
two. Which calls are visible to a tracer is a platform property rather than a
program property: Linux serves clock_gettime from the vDSO and FreeBSD from
its timekeeping page, so time.live is empty on both for a reason neither
shares with the other, while getrandom is a real syscall on Linux and
arc4random_buf is a userspace generator on FreeBSD that reseeds from the
kernel at most once.

PROVENANCE, because it differs and pretending otherwise would be the kind of
thing this project writes tiers to prevent:

  linux/    measured, with `sh tools/bscalls.sh --record`, on ubuntu-26.04.
  freebsd/  NOT YET MEASURED as of M3. Written from what the platform
            documents. The four empty files are certain -- none of those
            paths reaches the kernel on any platform, and rand.seeded's
            emptiness is a property of det.c rather than of FreeBSD.
            rand.live is the inference: arc4random_buf seeds its per-thread
            ChaCha state from the kernel on first use, which should be one
            getrandom. If the gate says otherwise, the gate is right; the
            failure prints the observed multiset and the fix is this line.

To re-pin a platform after a deliberate change: sh tools/bscalls.sh --record,
and say in the commit message what moved and why.
